#import "/modern-iaf/manuals/text/en/f35i/ch2/src.typ": *

Before the first switch of the checklist card, the pilot of a real jet checks the aircraft from the outside and the cockpit on climbing in. The sim has none of this: it is #term("not modelled"), and the jet stands ready with its canopy open.

The F-35's checklist is not public. What is public is a United States Air Force instruction with rules for the ground#afi-full[paragraphs 3.1 to 3.3]; the list below holds its items. The walk-around itself is inferred, as on any jet: no leaks, no damage, covers and pins removed.

- *Nothing loose.* Nothing lies where the intakes can swallow it or where it can block the stick or the throttle, and loose things in the cockpit are stowed before the engine starts with the canopy open#afi[paragraphs 3.1.1, 3.1.2 and 3.3.1].
- *The seat.* The survival kit's switch on the ejection seat is at its automatic position#afi[paragraph 3.1.3].
- *The ground crew.* Pilot and crew talk over the intercom for the start and the checks before taxi, and with hand signals otherwise. Each action on a system starts with the pilot; the crew repeats the signal when it is safe#afi[paragraph 3.2].
- *The helmet.* The pilot tests and adjusts the helmet's display before the flight#afi[paragraph 3.19.2]: in this jet it is the head-up display (@instruments).
- *Ice.* On a cold, wet day someone watches the intake for ice while the jet stands with its engine running; ice seen there means an engine shutdown at once#afi[paragraph 3.3.2].

#in-sim[The jet stands parked, checked and with the canopy open. #sym.arrow.r *Nothing to do*: go on with @cold-start. Not modelled: the walk-around, the ground crew, the helmet's fitting, ice.]
